Television technology sends image information from one place to another.This lesson describes radio transmission, camera tubes, and picture tubes in early analog television, not a single design shared by all modern television systems.In the analog television system described here, the transmitter first turns an image into an electrical signal that varies continuously with time and uses it to modulate a high-frequency oscillation.The modulated electromagnetic wave carries the image information to a distant receiver, which detects the image signal and converts it back into a visible image.Moving images are formed from successive pictures: dozens of slightly different pictures are transmitted each second to present motion;this description does not require every system to use the same picture rate.The textbook's **photoelectric camera tube** converts an image into a sequence of electrical signals (Figure 125).An optical system projects the object's image onto a mosaic screen made of many light-sensitive elements, whose charge states depend on the light they receive.The electron gun sends a beam across the elements of one row and then the next.When the beam reaches an element, its charge changes and produces a pulse of current through resistor $R$.In the textbook's approximate model, the charge change determines the current, and the voltage changes across resistor $R$ are proportional to the illumination changes along each scanned row.Brightness differences at different image positions thus become electrical signals arranged in scanning order.Figure 125The receiver obtains the **image signal** after detection.In the textbook's **electron-beam picture tube**, a control electrode changes the number of electrons in the beam and therefore the screen's brightness.Horizontal and vertical deflection coils sweep the beam across the screen, corresponding to the transmitter's scan of the camera tube's mosaic screen.Synchronization signals coordinate the timing and positions of the transmitting and receiving scans so that each brightness signal is placed at the correct image position.These camera and picture tubes are examples of early television equipment;
modern cameras and screens do not all use electron-beam scanning.The textbook discusses terrestrial television broadcasting using ultrashort radio waves, including the meter-wave band;
television signals are not restricted to this band.Such propagation generally depends mainly on line of sight, so higher antennas and relay transmitters can extend the receiving area.The textbook uses Moscow's Ostankino television tower, with a height of 540 meters and a receiving radius of 120 kilometers, as a historical example of the role of a tall tower, not a guarantee for every weather condition or location.Its account of the television network at that time mentions over a thousand broadcasting stations and tens of millions of television receivers;
these are not present-day statistics.Relay satellites can also extend television broadcasting coverage, and satellite relays use a different propagation path from terrestrial broadcasts.

